The three facets
description: value, keyed, ordinal - the capability cube and its corners
- '*Status: design - the facet keywords and their quantifiers are not implemented yet; the kept JSON-Schema keywords apply today.*'
- 'Every node is described by three independent **facets** - capabilities it may exercise:'
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
  header:
    - facet
    - keyword
    - meaning
      width: 2
  - ['**value**', '`value:`', 'a scalar **self-value** - the [value axis](*::language:model:values)']
  - ['**keyed**', '`keyed:`', 'named entries `key: ...`']
  - ['**ordinal**', '`ordinal:`', 'positional entries `- ...`']
- >
  In a schema, a facet keyword takes a **sub-schema**, or `false` to forbid the facet; omitting it
  leaves the facet allowed - open. The empty schema `{}` forbids nothing: that is `true`, the top.
  Each facet in {forbidden, allowed} gives the 2³ cube, and ordering the corners by capability-subset
  *is* the subtype lattice - `000` at the bottom, `111` at the top. The named kinds are shorthands for
  corners:
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
  header:
    - kind
    - facet record
      width: 3
    - bits
  - ['`null`', '`{value: {const: ~}, keyed: false, ordinal: false}`', '`000`']
  - ['`leaf` - a value alone (`str`, `int`, `binary`, ...)', '`{value: {type: ...}, keyed: false, ordinal: false}`', '`A00`']
  - ['`map` (was `object`)', '`{value: false, ordinal: false}`', '`010`']
  - ['`seq` (was `array`)', '`{value: false, keyed: false}`', '`001`']
  - ['`kseq` - a sequence with keys (the `!!mix` shape, was `mixed`)', '`{value: false, keyed: {min: 1}, ordinal: {min: 1}}`', '`011`']
  - ['`omni` (`!!yo`, was `variant`) ≡ `true`', '`{}`', '`111`']
  - ['`vmap` - a map with a scalar value', '`{ordinal: false}`', '`110`']
  - ['`vseq` - a seq with a scalar value', '`{keyed: false}`', '`101`']
- >
  The long JSON-Schema spellings (`object`, `array`, `mixed`, `variant`, `string`, `integer`, ...)
  read forever as aliases; the short YAML-style names are canonical
  ([terse keywords](*::meta:principles:terse-keywords)). Note that closed `leaf` - a node with no
  members - and the *open* value facet differ exactly on omni nodes: the open reading includes a
  value that has grown entries.
- >
  **omni ≡ true - the top.** The "permits any shape" formula is a tautology: every node is
  omni. As a *schema*, `type: omni` constrains nothing - and the `!!yo` surface tag (formerly
  `!!var`) is no shape assertion either: as a type it is the top, and its real payload is the schema
  *exemption* - plain yamlover, not bound by the enclosing document's meta
  ([mixtures](*::language:vs-yaml:differences:mixtures)). (The query matcher `!!<type: omni>` deliberately tests
  the *region* - a scalar that has own fields - a tautological test would select everything; the
  schema reading and the matcher reading are both correct, each on its own side.)
- >
  **Presence is not permission.** The facet bit says what *may* exist; element counts say what *does*.
  The quantifiers keep the two apart:
- !!<format: text/plain> |
  all keyed      →  ordinal: false      # no ordinal element may exist
  exists keyed   →  keyed:   {min: 1}
  all indexed    →  keyed:   false
  empty          →  keyed: false, ordinal: false
- >
  So `map` permits keyed and forbids ordinal - "all keyed", the empty node included vacuously -
  while `kseq` *requires* at least one entry of each.
- >
  **The bottom collapses.** The surface offers exactly three empties - `a:` (≡ `a: ~`, null: the bare
  key IS null), `a: {}`, and `a: []` - and no syntax for an "empty container that is neither", so the
  abstract no-value, no-keyed, no-ordinal corner has no writable inhabitant. An empty map, an empty
  seq, and that corner are one predicate with three spellings; the meet of `map` and `seq` is
  the bottom, and yamlover names it `null` - the one wart, inherent to `a:` meaning null. Which face
  an empty container shows JSON (`{}` vs `[]`) is a serialization hint, not a type fact.
- 'YAML''s extra collection "types" are refinements of the facets, not new corners:'
- !!<format: text/plain> |
  binary     → {value: {type: binary, format: image/png},
                keyed: false, ordinal: false}    # value axis
  timestamp  → {value: {type: str, format: date-time},
                keyed: false, ordinal: false}    # value axis
  set        → {value: false,
                ordinal: {uniqueItems: true}}    # a dedup FLAG
  omap       → {value: false, ordinal: false}    # = map - maps are
                                                 #   ALREADY ordered
  pairs      → {value: false, keyed: false,      # ordered [k, v]s;
                ordinal: {items: ...}}             #   keys may repeat
- >
  A facet carries a small fixed set of modifier keywords beyond false-or-schema - `uniqueItems` (set),
  key-uniqueness (omap versus pairs), ordering (yamlover's default) - almost all kept verbatim from
  JSON Schema. The merge key `<<` and YAML's `!!value` are mechanisms, not types. And `!!omap`,
  `!!pairs`, `!!binary` are more than refinements - reserved [transform names](*::transform), the
  records above being only their *result* shapes.
- >
  **The cube's schema spelling.** The facet keywords are the theory; today's authored spelling
  covers the same ground with `value:` for the value facet and the
  [members clause](*::meta:members) for the other two: keyed clauses and keyless clauses of ONE
  omni `members:` describe named and positional members respectively - matching the model, where
  both live in one ordered stream - and the sibling `others:` sweeps whatever no clause matched.
